      "message": "Narrow what owning an agent lets you do\n\nLetting a person have agents of their own widened three doors further\nthan intended, and an audit of the change found all three.\n\nA session credential could mint a standing token. Authority is the one\nplace a scope is consulted, and both of the shortcuts that skip it — it\nis your own, or you hold the agent — reached their answer another way.\nSo a credential drawn for one task's work, good for fifteen minutes,\ncould draw a permanent one carrying every grant its principal holds, for\nitself or for any agent its holder owns. Minting a standing token and\nregistering a principal now refuse a scope outright, and running the\nforge is no longer something a scoped credential can be: a session is\nnever an admin, which is also what let one read anybody's quota.\n\nBelonging to an organisation asked no capability of you. Acting for an\norganisation you are in is deliberately free — that is what membership\nmeans — but registering a principal is not one of the things membership\nshould buy, and an agent that merely sat on a team could make more\nagents and mint their tokens holding no grant at all. Registering a\nprincipal now asks what creating a repository asks: be a person, or hold\nthe grant that running the forge consists of.\n\nDeactivation was reversible by the party it was aimed at. Retiring an\nagent you hold is yours to do and stays yours; bringing one back is\nwhoever runs the forge's, because an undo in the hands of the agent's\nowner is no lever at all. And an owner that cannot hold anything now\nconfers nothing: a log from before agents were owned can name an agent\nas an owner, and a principal can be deactivated after the fact, so\nholding is checked against who the owner is now.\n\nChange-Id: Iauthority0101\nSigned-off-by: Mandip Adhikari <92096340+mandipadk@users.noreply.github.com>",
